## Summary
The article details a historical system in the United States where mail was delivered underground using pneumatic tubes, achieving speeds of 35 MPH from 1893 to 1953. It opens by referencing Senator Ted Stevens' analogy of the internet as 'a series of tubes,' which may be inspired by this early infrastructure.

## Key Points
- The US Mail operated an underground pneumatic tube network for mail delivery between 1893 and 1953.
- The system enabled mail transport at speeds up to 35 MPH, representing an early rapid communication infrastructure.
- Senator Ted Stevens' internet analogy draws historical parallels to pneumatic tube technology.
- The pneumatic tube network was a significant engineering achievement of its era.
- The system was phased out over time, likely replaced by more modern technologies.


